## Common Mistakes with ASP.NET Core Minimal APIs

| # | Mistake | Why it’s a problem | How to avoid it |
|---|---------|--------------------|-----------------|
| 1 | **Mixing MVC, Razor Pages and Minimal APIs in the same project** | The routing pipeline gets confused; the order of middlewares matters. | Keep each style in its own project or at least keep the *Program.cs* focused on one style. Use **endpoint groups** (`MapGroup`) if you need a mix of minimal APIs inside a classic project. |
| 2 | **Putting business logic directly in the lambda** | Lambdas become huge, hard to unit‑test, and violate single‑responsibility. | Move logic to a service class registered in DI (`builder.Services.AddScoped<IOrderService, OrderService>()`). Use the service inside the endpoint. |
| 3 | **Not registering required services** (e.g. `AddDbContext`, `AddHealthChecks`, `AddAuthentication`) | Endpoints throw `NullReferenceException` or “No authentication handler” errors at runtime. | Register all dependencies **before** calling `Map...` and run `builder.Build()`. Verify with a simple test that `app.Services.GetRequiredService<T>()` succeeds. |
| 4 | **Ignoring the order of middleware** (`UseRouting`, `UseCors`, `UseAuthentication`, `UseAuthorization`) | Endpoints can be unreachable or bypass authentication. | Follow the canonical order: `UseRouting() → UseCors() → UseAuthentication() → UseAuthorization() → UseEndpoints()`. |
| 5 | **Blocking I/O or using `.Result`/`.Wait()` in handlers** | Thread pool exhaustion → performance degradation. | Always `await` async methods. If you must call a sync API, offload it with `Task.Run` *only* for CPU‑bound work. |
| 6 | **Using static/global state in endpoints** | Shared state is not thread‑safe → race conditions, data corruption. | Use DI‑registered services (scoped, singleton, transient) or local variables. |
| 7 | **No or inadequate validation** | Bad input leads to 500 errors, data corruption, or open‑to‑injection vulnerabilities. | Use model binding + `[Required]`, `[Range]`, `[FromBody]` and check `result.Validate()` (e.g., FluentValidation). Return `ProblemDetails` on failure. |
| 8 | **Not using Swagger/OpenAPI** | Clients cannot discover endpoints, documentation is missing. | Call `builder.Services.AddEndpointsApiExplorer();` and `builder.Services.AddSwaggerGen();`. In `app`, add `app.UseSwagger()` and `app.UseSwaggerUI()`. |
| 9 | **Over‑loading routes without distinguishing HTTP verbs** | Ambiguous route resolution → 404 or 405. | Use `MapGet`, `MapPost`, `MapPut`, etc., and keep route templates unique. |
| 10 | **Missing health checks** | Production deployments cannot be monitored. | `builder.Services.AddHealthChecks();` and `app.MapHealthChecks("/health");`. |
| 11 | **No error handling middleware** | Unhandled exceptions leak stack traces. | Use `app.UseExceptionHandler("/error");` (or the minimal API style `app.UseExceptionHandler(e => ...)`). Return `ProblemDetails` consistently. |
| 12 | **Hard‑coding values** (connection strings, secrets, URLs) | Security risk & no environment support. | Use `builder.Configuration` + `IOptions<T>` or `IConfiguration.GetSection`. Add `appsettings.{Environment}.json`. |
| 13 | **Not configuring HTTPS / HSTS** | HTTP requests can be sniffed. | `app.UseHttpsRedirection();` and `app.UseHsts();` in non‑development. |
| 14 | **Not setting up CORS correctly** | Cross‑origin requests are blocked. | `builder.Services.AddCors(...)` and `app.UseCors("MyPolicy")`. |
| 15 | **Using lambda parameters for many dependencies** | Lame code smell, hard to read, and no DI benefits. | Prefer explicit service parameters: `app.MapPost("/items", async (ItemDto dto, IItemRepository repo) => { … })`. |
| 16 | **Inadequate logging** | Hard to debug production issues. | Register `ILogger<T>` via DI and use structured logging (`logger.LogInformation("User {Id} created item", userId);`). |
| 17 | **No or insufficient testing** | Endpoints may regress unnoticed. | Write integration tests with `WebApplicationFactory<T>` and unit tests for service layers. |
| 18 | **Using minimal APIs for features that need MVC** (Views, complex model binding, filters) | You lose those capabilities. | Stick to minimal APIs for pure HTTP APIs. Use MVC when you need filters, model state, view rendering, etc. |
| 19 | **Missing health checks for external dependencies** | You can’t detect upstream failures. | Register `builder.Services.AddHealthChecks().AddSqlServer(connectionString)` etc. |
| 20 | **Not using typed `HttpClient`** | You’ll create new clients per request or forget to use Polly policies. | `builder.Services.AddHttpClient<IFooClient, FooClient>();` and inject `IFooClient`. |

---

## How to Avoid These Pitfalls – A Practical Checklist

1. **Project Setup**

   ```csharp
   var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);

   // 1. Services
   builder.Services.AddDbContext<AppDbContext>(options => /*…*/);
   builder.Services.AddScoped<IOrderService, OrderService>();
   builder.Services.AddHealthChecks();
   builder.Services.AddAuthentication();
   builder.Services.AddAuthorization();
   builder.Services.AddCors(options =>
   {
       options.AddPolicy("AllowAll",
           b => b.AllowAnyOrigin()
                 .AllowAnyMethod()
                 .AllowAnyHeader());
   });

   // 2. Swagger
   builder.Services.AddEndpointsApiExplorer();
   builder.Services.AddSwaggerGen();

   // 3. Typed HttpClient
   builder.Services.AddHttpClient<IFooClient, FooClient>(c =>
   {
       c.BaseAddress = new Uri("https://api.foo.com");
   });

   var app = builder.Build();

   // 4. Middleware pipeline
   if (app.Environment.IsDevelopment())
   {
       app.UseSwagger();
       app.UseSwaggerUI();
   }
   else
   {
       app.UseHsts();
   }

   app.UseHttpsRedirection();
   app.UseCors("AllowAll");
   app.UseAuthentication();
   app.UseAuthorization();

   // 5. Global error handling
   app.UseExceptionHandler(errorApp =>
   {
       errorApp.Run(async context =>
       {
           var exception = context.Features.Get<IExceptionHandlerFeature>()?.Error;
           var problem = new ProblemDetails
           {
               Status = StatusCodes.Status500InternalServerError,
               Title = "An unexpected error occurred.",
               Detail = exception?.Message
           };
           context.Response.StatusCode = StatusCodes.Status500InternalServerError;
           await context.Response.WriteAsJsonAsync(problem);
       });
   });

   // 6. Health check endpoint
   app.MapHealthChecks("/health");
   ```

2. **Endpoint Definitions**

   ```csharp
   // Grouped endpoints with tags for Swagger
   var items = app.MapGroup("/api/items")
       .WithTags("Items")
       .RequireAuthorization();

   items.MapGet("/", async (IItemRepository repo) =>
   {
       var items = await repo.GetAllAsync();
       return Results.Ok(items);
   });

   items.MapGet("/{id:int}", async (int id, IItemRepository repo) =>
   {
       var item = await repo.GetByIdAsync(id);
       return item is null ? Results.NotFound() : Results.Ok(item);
   });

   items.MapPost("/", async (ItemDto dto, IItemRepository repo) =>
   {
       if (!Validator.TryValidate(dto, out var errors))
       {
           return Results.ValidationProblem(errors);
       }

       var created = await repo.CreateAsync(dto);
       return Results.Created($"/api/items/{created.Id}", created);
   });
   ```

3. **Validation Example (FluentValidation)**

   ```csharp
   public class ItemDtoValidator : AbstractValidator<ItemDto>
   {
       public ItemDtoValidator()
       {
           RuleFor(x => x.Name).NotEmpty().MaximumLength(100);
           RuleFor(x => x.Price).GreaterThan(0);
       }
   }

   // Register
   builder.Services.AddValidatorsFromAssemblyContaining<ItemDtoValidator>();
   ```

   In the endpoint:

   ```csharp
   items.MapPost("/", async (ItemDto dto, IValidator<ItemDto> validator, IItemRepository repo) =>
   {
       var validationResult = await validator.ValidateAsync(dto);
       if (!validationResult.IsValid)
           return Results.ValidationProblem(validationResult.ToDictionary());

       var created = await repo.CreateAsync(dto);
       return Results.Created($"/api/items/{created.Id}", created);
   });
   ```

4. **Async‑only Code**

   ```csharp
   items.MapGet("/", async (IItemRepository repo) => await repo.GetAllAsync());
   ```

5. **Logging**

   ```csharp
   items.MapPost("/", async (ItemDto dto, IItemRepository repo, ILogger<Program> logger) =>
   {
       logger.LogInformation("Creating item {Name}", dto.Name);
       // …
   });
   ```

6. **Testing (xUnit + WebApplicationFactory)**

   ```csharp
   public class ItemsEndpointTests : IClassFixture<WebApplicationFactory<Program>>
   {
       private readonly HttpClient _client;

       public ItemsEndpointTests(WebApplicationFactory<Program> factory)
       {
           _client = factory.CreateClient();
       }

       [Fact]
       public async Task GetItems_ReturnsOk()
       {
           var response = await _client.GetAsync("/api/items");
           response.EnsureSuccessStatusCode();
           var content = await response.Content.ReadFromJsonAsync<List<ItemDto>>();
           Assert.NotEmpty(content);
       }
   }
   ```

