## Quick Reference – “Avoid These” Checklist

| ✅ | Action |
|----|--------|
| 1 | **Use DI** – never new‑up services in lambdas. |
| 2 | **Register all services before mapping endpoints.** |
| 3 | **Keep the middleware pipeline in canonical order.** |
| 4 | **Always `await` async calls; no `.Result` or `.Wait()`.** |
| 5 | **No static/shared mutable state.** |
| 6 | **Validate input; return `ProblemDetails`.** |
| 7 | **Add Swagger/OpenAPI.** |
| 8 | **Avoid duplicate routes; keep verbs separate.** |
| 9 | **Add health checks.** |
|10 | **Configure HTTPS & HSTS.** |
|11 | **Configure CORS correctly.** |
|12 | **Use structured logging.** |
|13 | **Unit‑test services; integration‑test endpoints.** |
|14 | **Use typed HttpClient for external calls.** |
|15 | **Use `IOptions<T>` for config.** |
|16 | **Use `app.MapGroup` for logical grouping & tags.** |
|17 | **Avoid over‑engineering with filters; use endpoint filters if needed.** |
|18 | **Prefer MVC controllers when you need views, filters, or complex model binding.** |
|19 | **Add global exception handling middleware.** |
|20 | **Use Swagger tags to keep API docs readable.** |

---

### Bottom‑Line

Minimal APIs are an excellent fit for lightweight services, micro‑services, or quick prototypes. The real key to success is **keeping the same design principles you’d use with MVC**: separation of concerns, dependency injection, validation, logging, error handling, and testing. Once those patterns are in place, the brevity of minimal APIs will let you ship features faster while still maintaining maintainability, security, and observability.
